<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Dahae</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederation of Iranian nomads east of the Caspian whose Parni tribe founded the Parthian dynasty that would rule Iran for nearly five centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Dahae appear in a daiva inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> and in Greek accounts of the Achaemenid army, living on the steppes south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>. They comprised three main tribes, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parni">Parni</a>, Xanthii and Pissuri, and are often counted among the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/saka">Saka</a> peoples.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They were horse-riding pastoralists famed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a>, serving as cavalry for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-iii">Darius III</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a> and later for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and his successors. Their territory, later called Dahistan, bordered the farming lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcania">Hyrcania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margiana">Margiana</a>, and they traded and raided along this edge.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 238 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsaces-i">Arsaces</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parni">Parni</a>, seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsacid-dynasty">Arsacid dynasty</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>, whose era was later reckoned from 247 BCE. The remaining Dahae were absorbed into Parthian and later steppe populations, though the name Dahistan survived for the region into Islamic times.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/dahae.jpg" alt="Dahae" width="268" height="206"><figcaption>Image: Dogaru Florin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confedera%C5%A3ia.Dahae.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arsaces I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iranian steppe religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Eastern Iranian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>, <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dahae confederacy, Daae</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-350&amp;focus=dahae" aria-label="Dahae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6126 751 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dahae lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M6141 1232h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6446 1007h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6446 1007h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Turkmenistan. <a href="/atlas?year=-350&amp;focus=dahae">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
